.TH NCURSES 3 "2026" "y-- standard library" "y-- Library Reference"
.SH NAME
Ncurses \- Terminal UI, screen management, and keyboard handling
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B Ncurses
module provides a direct mapping of the standard C ncurses API to the y-- language.
This allows developers to build complex text-based user interfaces and handle terminal
input/output in a platform-independent way (on systems where ncurses is supported).
.PP
Functions that return or accept WINDOW pointers in C represent them as integers
in y--. The stdscr is implicitly used by many functions, but for specific windows,
store the integer returned by \fBinitscr()\fR or \fBnewwin()\fR and pass it to functions
prefixed with 'w' or 'mvw'.
.PP
Import examples:
    import Ncurses
.SH "INITIALIZATION & CLEANUP"
.TP
\fBinitscr()\fR -> int()
Initializes the ncurses system, sets up the standard screen (stdscr), configures
cbreak, noecho, and keypad modes, and returns the stdscr pointer cast to an integer.
.TP
\fBendwin()\fR -> none()
Ends ncurses mode, tears down the active windows, and restores the terminal to its
normal state. Must be called before the program exits.
.TP
\fBisendwin()\fR -> bool()
Returns true if endwin() has been called without a subsequent refresh().
.SH "COLORS AND ATTRIBUTES"
.PP
.B Constants (Attributes):
.br
A_NORMAL, A_BOLD, A_UNDERLINE, A_REVERSE, A_BLINK
.PP
.B Constants (Colors):
.br
COLOR_BLACK, COLOR_RED, COLOR_GREEN, COLOR_YELLOW, COLOR_BLUE, COLOR_MAGENTA,
COLOR_CYAN, COLOR_WHITE
.TP
\fBstart_color()\fR -> int()
Initializes color support in the terminal. Must be called before color functions.
.TP
\fBhas_colors()\fR -> bool()
Returns true if the terminal supports colors.
.TP
\fBinit_pair(pair, f, b)\fR -> int()
Initializes a color pair with ID `pair` using foreground `f` and background `b`.
.TP
\fBCOLOR_PAIR(n)\fR -> int()
Returns the attribute value for the specified color pair n.
.TP
\fBattron(attr)\fR, \fBattroff(attr)\fR, \fBattrset(attr)\fR -> int()
Turns on, turns off, or sets the current text attributes.
.SH "OUTPUT / PRINTING"
.TP
\fBprintw(str)\fR -> none()
Prints the string \fBstr\fR to the standard screen at the current cursor position.
.TP
\fBmvprintw(y, x, str)\fR -> none()
Moves the cursor to row \fBy\fR and column \fBx\fR, then prints \fBstr\fR.
.TP
\fBwprintw(win, str)\fR -> none()
Prints \fBstr\fR to the specified window \fBwin\fR.
.TP
\fBmvwprintw(win, y, x, str)\fR -> none()
Moves the cursor in window \fBwin\fR and prints \fBstr\fR.
.TP
\fBaddnstr/mvaddnstr/waddnstr/mvwaddnstr\fR -> int()
Writes at most n characters of a string.
.TP
\fBhline/vline\fR (and mv/w/mvw variants) -> int()
Draws a horizontal or vertical line using the specified character for a given length.
.TP
\fBbox(win, verch, horch)\fR -> int()
Draws a box around the edges of the window \fBwin\fR.
.SH "INPUT"
.TP
\fBgetch()\fR -> int()
Reads a single character or key code (like KEY_UP) from the standard screen.
.TP
\fBmvgetch(y, x)\fR, \fBwgetch(win)\fR, \fBmvwgetch(win, y, x)\fR -> int()
Variants of getch for specific positions and windows.
.TP
\fBtimeout(delay)\fR -> none()
Sets the blocking delay for getch(). Positive values delay for `delay` milliseconds.
Zero makes it non-blocking. Negative makes it infinitely blocking.
.TP
\fBkeypad(win, bf)\fR -> int()
Enables (bf=true) or disables (bf=false) the keypad of the user's terminal for \fBwin\fR.
.SH "SCREEN, WINDOW, AND CURSOR MANAGEMENT"
.TP
\fBrefresh()\fR, \fBwrefresh(win)\fR -> int()
Updates the physical terminal screen to match the internal ncurses buffer.
.TP
\fBclear()\fR, \fBerase()\fR, \fBwclear(win)\fR, \fBwerase(win)\fR -> int()
Clears the screen or window. clear() also forces a full redraw on the next refresh.
.TP
\fBmove(y, x)\fR, \fBwmove(win, y, x)\fR -> int()
Moves the logical cursor to the given coordinates.
.TP
\fBgetmaxy()\fR, \fBgetmaxx()\fR -> int()
Returns the maximum Y (height) and X (width) of the standard screen.
.TP
\fBgetyx()\fR -> list()
Returns a list [y, x] containing the current cursor position on stdscr.
.TP
\fBcurs_set(visibility)\fR -> int()
Sets cursor visibility: 0 (invisible), 1 (normal), or 2 (very visible).
.TP
\fBnewwin(nlines, ncols, begin_y, begin_x)\fR -> int()
Creates and returns a new window with the specified dimensions and position.
.TP
\fBdelwin(win)\fR -> int()
Deletes the window \fBwin\fR, freeing its memory.
.SH "TERMINAL MODES"
.TP
\fBcbreak() / nocbreak()\fR -> int()
Enables or disables cbreak mode (characters are passed immediately to the program).
.TP
\fBecho() / noecho()\fR -> int()
Enables or disables echoing of typed characters.
.SH "MISCELLANEOUS"
.TP
\fBbeep()\fR, \fBflash()\fR -> int()
Sounds the terminal bell or flashes the screen.
.TP
\fBnapms(ms)\fR -> int()
Sleeps for \fBms\fR milliseconds.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
Raylib(3), System(3)
